name: tls and irc
boss: Rebase
flavor: Your history is mine to rewrite.
brief: Everything nc carries is plaintext -- readable and editable by anyone on
brief: the path. tlstunnel is this system's answer: it wraps a plain connection
brief: in TLS, checking the server's certificate against trust anchors compiled
brief: into the binary itself, not read from a file some attacker could edit.
brief:
brief: ii is irc as a directory tree. Each channel is a folder with an `out`
brief: file you read and an `in` fifo you write to. No client, no ui -- which
brief: means tail, grep and echo are the client.
---
q: open a tls connection to @host@ on port 443 and hand it to stdin and stdout
a: tlstunnel - @host@ 443
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
---
q: fetch a page over https by speaking http into a tls tunnel
a: printf 'HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: @host@\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n' | tlstunnel - @host@ 443
ref: tlstunnel
teach: tlstunnel
---
q: count the trust anchors compiled into the tunnel binary
a: strings /bin/tlstunnel | grep -c 'Internet Security Research Group'
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: strings
teach: strings
hint: they are in the binary, not in a file -- there is nothing on disk to edit
---
q: start ii against @host@ on the standard tls-free irc port, as nick vos
a: ii -s @host@ -p 6667 -n vos -i $HOME/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -s
teach: ii -p
teach: ii -n
---
q: start ii with a chosen real name
a: ii -s @host@ -f vos -n vos -i $HOME/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -f
---
q: start ii putting its directory tree somewhere you choose
a: ii -s @host@ -n vos -i /tmp/irc
ref: ii
teach: ii -i
---
q: join the channel #vos by writing to the server's in fifo
a: echo '/j #vos' > $HOME/irc/@host@/in
ref: ii
teach: ii
---
q: read what is happening in a channel, live
a: tail -f $HOME/irc/@host@/#vos/out
ref: tail
teach: tail -f
---
q: say hello in the channel
a: echo hello > $HOME/irc/@host@/#vos/in
ref: ii
teach: ii
---
q: find every mention of your nick across every channel
a: grep -r vos $HOME/irc
ref: grep
teach: grep -r
---
q: list every channel ii has joined
a: find $HOME/irc -maxdepth 2 -type d -name '#*'
ref: find
teach: find -type
---
setup: Remote access on vos works by dialing OUT to a machine you control over
setup: wireguard, so vos never listens on the network it is plugged into. These
setup: are the pieces. None can run in this practice sandbox, so they are graded
setup: on what you type.
q: generate a new wireguard private key and save it to private.key
a: wg genkey > private.key
ref: wg
teach: wg
---
q: derive the public key from private.key
a: wg pubkey < private.key
ref: wg
teach: wg
---
q: create a wireguard interface called wg0
a: ip link add wg0 type wireguard
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: give wg0 the address 10.9.0.2 in a /24
a: ip addr add 10.9.0.2/24 dev wg0
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: bring wg0 up
a: ip link set wg0 up
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: set wg0's private key from private.key
a: wg set wg0 private-key private.key
ref: wg
teach: wg
---
q: add a peer to wg0 by its public key, endpoint and allowed ips
a: wg set wg0 peer PUBKEY endpoint titan.example:51820 allowed-ips 10.9.0.0/24
ref: wg
teach: wg
---
q: show the state of every wireguard interface
a: wg show
ref: wg
teach: wg
---
q: make an ed25519 ssh key at /state/id
a: dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f /state/id
ref: dropbearkey
teach: dropbearkey -t
teach: dropbearkey -f
---
q: print the public half of /state/id, to hand to the other machine
a: dropbearkey -y -f /state/id
ref: dropbearkey
teach: dropbearkey -y
---
q: run the ssh server with an ed25519 host key, bound only to the wireguard address
a: dropbear -r /state/host_key -p 10.9.0.1:22
ref: dropbear
teach: dropbear -r
teach: dropbear -p
---
q: run the ssh server in the foreground, logging to stderr
a: dropbear -r /state/host_key -F -E
ref: dropbear
teach: dropbear -F
teach: dropbear -E
---
q: ssh out to root at 10.9.0.1 using the key /state/id
a: dbclient -i /state/id root@10.9.0.1
ref: dbclient
teach: dbclient -i
---
q: ssh in and attach to a running abduco session in one command
a: dbclient -i /state/id root@10.9.0.1 'abduco -a work'
ref: dbclient
teach: dbclient
hint: the whole point -- reconnect straight into the work that survived the drop
---
q: ssh to a host on a non-standard port
a: dbclient -p 2222 root@10.9.0.1
ref: dbclient
teach: dbclient -p
---
q: forward local port 8080 to a service on the far side, over ssh
a: dbclient -L 8080:localhost:80 root@10.9.0.1
ref: dbclient
teach: dbclient -L
